## 5. Layer 3 — procedures, and how `rm` and `curl | bash` still get through

The earlier draft proposed a closed operation catalog. That is wrong as a *hard wall*: real tasks legitimately need deletion and legitimately need privileged shell execution — the official NodeSource Node.js install genuinely *is* `curl -fsSL https://deb.nodesource.com/setup_20.x | sudo -E bash -`. A rule that bans it fails family 6 outright and buys nothing, because the attacker's payload in that family is not "uses bash" but "uses bash *for something the user never asked for*".

So Layer 3 is restructured as **parse → classify → contain → an escape ladder**, with the closed catalog demoted from "the only path" to "the best path".

### 5.1 Parse: procedures become ASTs, never strings

An untrusted-proposed procedure is parsed host-side (`shlex`/`bashlex`) into a typed AST — argv arrays, pipelines, redirections, `&&`/`;` sequences — where every **leaf** is a Layer-1 typed value with its own label, and every **node** carries a capability. It is never handled as a string, and the string the untrusted source wrote is never what executes: the host **re-renders** an argv/pipeline from the verified AST. Parameter metacharacters are therefore inert by construction, and this also sidesteps the `template`-splicing path implicated in the literal-laundering note.

Parse failure ⇒ reject. An unparseable procedure is not a procedure the agent may run.

### 5.2 Classify: nodes → capabilities

Reuse `capabilities.py`, which already has the classifier and is currently unused on the SA path: `CAPABILITY_CATALOG` (28 capabilities, each with a `Tier`) plus `_CAPABILITY_SIGNALS`, which already maps `rm -rf`/`os.remove` → `os.fs.delete`, `curl … | sh`/`sudo`/`chmod` → `os.terminal.run`, file-open-for-write → `os.fs.write`. Move this from regex-over-a-string (where it is evadable) to classification-over-the-parsed-AST (where it is not), and treat any node it cannot classify as `UNKNOWN → DESTRUCTIVE`, matching `policies.CAP_TIER`'s existing default-deny for opaque affordances.

### 5.3 Contain: the effect set must fit the episode's authorized capability set

`EpisodeCapabilities` is derived **once, from the task instruction and starting-state note only**, exactly as `trust_detection.py` derives domain trust, and carries the same invariant: nothing read mid-episode can widen it. The AST's effect set must be a subset. This is P2, and it is the whole answer to "how does `rm` still get through":

| Task | Authorized set (from task text alone) | `rm -f ~/BankStatement.pdf` |
|---|---|---|
| "install a CPU monitor from this tutorial" | `os.terminal.run`, `os.fs.write` | **rejected** — `os.fs.delete` ∉ set |
| "clean up the build artifacts listed in this README" | `os.terminal.run`, `os.fs.write`, `os.fs.delete` | **admitted**, then constrained by §5.4 Tier B |

The measurable claim, and it holds across the benchmark: **every RTC-Bench payload requests a capability the benign task never authorized** — `rm -f` and SSH-key writes inside *install*/*clone* tasks, `truncate` of 10 000 files inside a *clone* task, adding a repository member inside a *comment* task. Containment kills them as a class, without any detector to evade.

### 5.4 The escape ladder: four ways an AST earns the right to run

Tried in order; the first that succeeds sets the resulting integrity level.

**Tier A — canonical playbook (best).** The host owns the procedure; the untrusted source supplies only parameters. "How do I install Node.js" should never be answered by a chat NPC; "*which version*" legitimately can be. Ops like `InstallPackage(manager, name, version?)`, `CloneRepo(url)`, `DownloadFile(url, dest)`, `SetConfig(scope, key, value)` render to fixed argv with Layer-1/Layer-2-verified leaves. → **TRUSTED** (procedure is the host's; only leaves were untrusted, and they were verified).

**Tier B — capability authorized by the task; parameters constrained.** No playbook, but the effect set fits (§5.3). The AST runs, but every leaf must independently clear:
* `FILE_PATH` leaves must lie under a **scope root** derived from the trusted side (the directory the task named), and must pass `task_match` or `candidate_set`;
* a **cardinality bound** on how many objects one procedure may affect (blocks "delete 10 000 files" even when deletion is authorized);
* recursion/globbing (`-r`, `*`) only if the task text itself authorized something recursive;
* `URL` leaves must clear the host allowlist; `PACKAGE_NAME` leaves must clear `registry`.
→ **CORROBORATED** (structure authorized by the user, parameters only partially verified).

**Tier C — structural crosscheck against an independent trusted source.** The AST's *shape* is not in any playbook and may be arbitrary (`curl … | sudo -E bash -`). It is admitted only if the **same AST can be independently re-derived from an allowlisted authoritative source** and compared **structurally, deterministically** — verb sequence, pipeline topology, redirection targets, and typed leaves (URL host, package name, version) — never by LLM judgement over prose. This is `crosscheck` lifted from scalars to procedures. → **TRUSTED** from an official source (`nodejs.org`), **CORROBORATED** from a corroborating one (a GitHub README).

The security statement this yields is the important one, and it is *not* "dangerous verbs are banned":

> **A procedure runs because a trusted source independently states that procedure. Any structure that cannot be re-derived from a trusted source is dropped — including structure appended to an otherwise-correct procedure.**

An injected extra pipeline stage or extra `&&` clause has no counterpart in the trusted derivation, so structural comparison rejects it *without needing to recognize it as malicious*.

**Tier D — nothing verifies.** Refuse, and report why. `--sa_trust_confirmation` remains the operator's only escalation, off by default per the no-human-in-the-loop stance.

### 5.5 Where this genuinely does not discriminate

When the benign task authorizes the same capability the injection wants, *and* the injected parameter falls inside the authorized scope root, containment and parameter checks both pass. §7.7 states that residual honestly and says what it costs.

